1
00:00:25,621 --> 00:00:29,001
Child of a Hero

2
00:00:43,931 --> 00:00:46,851
My mother will die soon.

3
00:00:48,111 --> 00:00:51,111
She said she wanted to return to her hometown.

4
00:00:52,531 --> 00:00:53,651
I brought her here.

5
00:02:33,171 --> 00:02:34,881
NOVEMBER 1013 AD
WESSEX, SOUTH OF ENGLAND

6
00:02:34,961 --> 00:02:39,511
Our main army
defeated the resistance at Wessex.

7
00:02:40,841 --> 00:02:44,931
It is only a matter of time until we conquer the land.

8
00:02:45,011 --> 00:02:49,271
If all goes well, everything will be done before winter.

9
00:02:50,851 --> 00:02:53,361
What happened to London?

10
00:02:55,651 --> 00:02:56,611
And Canute,

11
00:02:57,741 --> 00:02:59,951
where is he?

12
00:03:00,031 --> 00:03:01,241
Yes.

13
00:03:01,321 --> 00:03:06,991
The besieging army that Canute was leading
was attacked by Thorkell.

14
00:03:07,081 --> 00:03:10,001
We haven't heard anything since.

15
00:03:10,081 --> 00:03:12,961
I heard rumors they engaged in battle at Marlborough.

16
00:03:13,041 --> 00:03:16,501
It's possible they joined the local soldiers.

17
00:03:16,591 --> 00:03:20,171
However, we are not yet sure
whether they got away safely.

18
00:03:21,431 --> 00:03:23,721
He's my dear son.

19
00:03:23,801 --> 00:03:27,311
As soon as you find out more, tell me immediately.

20
00:03:27,391 --> 00:03:28,521
Yes, sir.

21
00:03:46,201 --> 00:03:47,371
What?

22
00:03:47,451 --> 00:03:49,121
We're surrounded!

23
00:03:49,201 --> 00:03:50,331
It can't be.

24
00:03:52,041 --> 00:03:54,501
See? I told you so!

25
00:03:54,581 --> 00:03:56,131
Damn!

26
00:03:56,211 --> 00:03:58,381
Calm down, men!

27
00:03:58,461 --> 00:04:02,301
Listen well! Don't return fire. Don't touch them!

28
00:04:04,971 --> 00:04:07,721
Messengers of Brycheiniog!

29
00:04:07,811 --> 00:04:11,231
What a rough welcome!

30
00:04:11,311 --> 00:04:15,941
So you speak our language, you barbarian.

31
00:04:16,021 --> 00:04:21,361
I heard that you Danes were just like beasts
that can't talk.

32
00:04:21,441 --> 00:04:24,241
Withdraw your troops, Asser.

33
00:04:24,321 --> 00:04:29,201
Making trouble with the Danes
is of no benefit to Wales.

34
00:04:29,281 --> 00:04:30,701
Gratianus!

35
00:04:30,791 --> 00:04:37,421
I respect your concern
for the future of the kingdoms of Wales.

36
00:04:37,501 --> 00:04:41,511
But our values are different from yours.

37
00:04:41,591 --> 00:04:46,301
What promise are you trying to make
with the prince of the Danes?

38
00:04:46,391 --> 00:04:49,601
They're just barbarians! They have no morals!

39
00:04:50,641 --> 00:04:52,771
What is he shouting about?

40
00:04:52,851 --> 00:04:55,641
Are we going to fight or not?

41
00:04:55,731 --> 00:04:58,981
No. This isn't going to turn into a battle.

42
00:04:59,061 --> 00:05:01,231
Do you understand what he's saying?

43
00:05:01,321 --> 00:05:03,781
No, I don't.

44
00:05:03,861 --> 00:05:07,531
But they could have killed one of us with their first shot
if they wanted to.

45
00:05:07,611 --> 00:05:10,241
They were trying to intimidate us

46
00:05:10,331 --> 00:05:13,871
so that they could control the negotiations.

47
00:05:13,951 --> 00:05:15,541
I see.

48
00:05:15,621 --> 00:05:17,791
You beasts!

49
00:05:17,881 --> 00:05:23,011
I watched your war with England closely.

50
00:05:23,091 --> 00:05:27,131
You entered three cease-fire agreements
in the last ten years,

51
00:05:27,221 --> 00:05:29,931
and violated them all!

52
00:05:30,011 --> 00:05:33,431
We will not make the same foolish mistake as England!

53
00:05:33,521 --> 00:05:37,191
We shall capture the prince and use him as a shield!

54
00:05:37,271 --> 00:05:41,321
Even the king of the devils
must still love his own child!

55
00:05:43,571 --> 00:05:45,691
Sorry, Askeladd.

56
00:05:45,781 --> 00:05:48,361
No. It's just an act.

57
00:05:48,451 --> 00:05:51,201
He's just making trouble to see how we'll respond.

58
00:05:52,281 --> 00:05:56,791
These days, a sheep must wear a wolf's skin to graze.

59
00:05:56,871 --> 00:05:59,251
I feel sympathy for them.

60
00:05:59,331 --> 00:06:02,751
Are you going to tell them about your plan?

61
00:06:02,841 --> 00:06:04,591
I could...

62
00:06:05,881 --> 00:06:10,681
but if they want to put up an act,
I can go along with them.

63
00:06:17,851 --> 00:06:20,191
Look closely!

64
00:06:20,271 --> 00:06:22,271
The man standing here

65
00:06:22,361 --> 00:06:25,611
is none other than the son of King Sweyn of Denmark,

66
00:06:25,691 --> 00:06:29,111
His Highness Canute.

67
00:06:29,201 --> 00:06:32,621
His Highness will now address you.

68
00:06:33,831 --> 00:06:36,041
Be silent and listen!

69
00:06:37,831 --> 00:06:41,501
All right, Your Highness. Say something spirited.

70
00:06:43,131 --> 00:06:46,591
It can be anything. Like, "I'm going to boil you
alive and eat you."

71
00:06:46,671 --> 00:06:50,091
Make those rude scoundrels uncomfortable.

72
00:06:52,341 --> 00:06:56,641
If it comes from you, their balls will shrink
and they'll let us through.

73
00:07:04,571 --> 00:07:05,611
Your Highness!

74
00:07:09,701 --> 00:07:13,531
His Highness dislikes talking in front of other people.

75
00:07:13,621 --> 00:07:14,621
I shall do it instead--

76
00:07:14,701 --> 00:07:16,991
No, Ragnar.

77
00:07:17,081 --> 00:07:18,371
What?

78
00:07:18,451 --> 00:07:21,211
He may be young, but he's still a prince.

79
00:07:21,291 --> 00:07:24,251
He has responsibilities he must live up to.

80
00:07:24,331 --> 00:07:26,631
Everyone standing here now

81
00:07:26,711 --> 00:07:29,671
is acting for the prince.

82
00:07:31,301 --> 00:07:33,551
He is a gentle person.

83
00:07:33,641 --> 00:07:36,261
He doesn't like unnecessary fighting.

84
00:07:36,351 --> 00:07:38,141
That's exactly why

85
00:07:38,221 --> 00:07:42,731
I'm asking him to do this.
To avoid an unnecessary fight!

86
00:07:42,811 --> 00:07:44,771
Do you think you can handle succeeding

87
00:07:44,861 --> 00:07:47,571
King Sweyn with your mouth clamped up like a clam?

88
00:07:47,651 --> 00:07:48,861
Silence!

89
00:07:50,321 --> 00:07:53,951
You could never understand His Highness!

90
00:07:55,371 --> 00:07:59,831
The palace is a fox nest where parents, children,
and siblings eat each other!

91
00:07:59,911 --> 00:08:05,631
Do you have any idea how hard it was for
the frail and sickly prince to survive through that?

92
00:08:05,711 --> 00:08:08,301
He silenced his heart, silenced his breath,

93
00:08:08,381 --> 00:08:11,631
and made sure he didn't stand out.
That's how he survived!

94
00:08:11,721 --> 00:08:15,721
And even after all that, he's still approached

95
00:08:15,801 --> 00:08:18,311
by bastards like you who want to use him!

96
00:08:24,481 --> 00:08:27,651
Don't step out of line.

97
00:08:27,731 --> 00:08:32,651
It's not your place to try to control how he acts.

98
00:08:40,451 --> 00:08:42,121
What's wrong?

99
00:08:42,201 --> 00:08:44,621
What are you arguing about?

100
00:08:44,711 --> 00:08:46,881
What does Canute say?

101
00:08:49,041 --> 00:08:50,551
Gratianus.

102
00:08:51,671 --> 00:08:53,761
I'm going to tell them the plan.

103
00:08:55,801 --> 00:08:58,601
Very well. I shall go too.

104
00:08:59,681 --> 00:09:02,521
Guys! Wait right there.

105
00:09:02,601 --> 00:09:05,521
I'm going to go talk to their leader.

106
00:09:05,601 --> 00:09:07,191
Okay.

107
00:09:08,941 --> 00:09:10,021
Ragnar.

108
00:09:11,531 --> 00:09:13,781
You are correct.

109
00:09:13,861 --> 00:09:16,071
I was worried about the prince,

110
00:09:16,151 --> 00:09:18,911
and ended up overstepping my bounds.

111
00:09:18,991 --> 00:09:22,161
Your Highness, please forgive me.

112
00:09:43,101 --> 00:09:46,691
You loser. Are you really the same age as me?

113
00:09:56,861 --> 00:09:59,781
That man is named Ragnar, right?

114
00:09:59,871 --> 00:10:01,281
Yes.

115
00:10:01,371 --> 00:10:05,331
He doesn't know that fledglings all leave
the nest someday.

116
00:10:05,411 --> 00:10:08,171
What he's doing is actually bad for the chick.

117
00:10:09,751 --> 00:10:14,591
There are many things you have to do
to put your plan in motion.

118
00:10:14,671 --> 00:10:17,221
The first is to make the chick leave the nest.

119
00:10:18,511 --> 00:10:21,221
Yes. I know.

120
00:10:32,691 --> 00:10:34,861
Stay back. I don't need any guards.

121
00:10:34,941 --> 00:10:37,071
But...

122
00:10:37,151 --> 00:10:39,071
Don't make me say it again.

123
00:10:39,151 --> 00:10:43,701
It would bring shame to our king if they thought
I can't talk without guards.

124
00:10:49,621 --> 00:10:53,251
More! Get five times further away!

125
00:10:53,341 --> 00:10:54,921
Tell the others too!

126
00:10:58,011 --> 00:11:00,091
All right.

127
00:11:00,181 --> 00:11:02,681
You said your name was Askeladd, right?

128
00:11:02,761 --> 00:11:07,141
I understand your situation,
but you're putting me in a hard spot.

129
00:11:07,221 --> 00:11:10,351
I heard that he's already agreed.

130
00:11:10,441 --> 00:11:15,771
You're asking for permission for passage
through our country and supplies for your men.

131
00:11:15,861 --> 00:11:18,611
I can't agree immediately.

132
00:11:18,691 --> 00:11:24,071
We may be small, but our king also has a reputation.

133
00:11:24,161 --> 00:11:28,581
What's in it for us if we help you?

134
00:11:28,661 --> 00:11:32,831
I need to confirm that this is a transaction between
two parties of equal standing.

135
00:11:32,921 --> 00:11:38,261
Or else my men will think that the king is scared
of only 100 Danes.

136
00:11:40,011 --> 00:11:41,761
You're right.

137
00:11:41,841 --> 00:11:47,101
It wouldn't look good for you
if you just agreed to our demands.

138
00:11:47,181 --> 00:11:49,931
I apologize for not showing more consideration.

139
00:11:50,021 --> 00:11:53,401
I'm the one who wrote the letter to your country.

140
00:11:53,481 --> 00:11:56,061
I thought I wrote in the letter that I can't give details

141
00:11:56,151 --> 00:11:59,441
about your reward
in order to maintain secrecy.

142
00:11:59,531 --> 00:12:01,321
Yes. I read that.

143
00:12:03,111 --> 00:12:07,031
Then may I ask about that secret?

144
00:12:09,241 --> 00:12:10,661
Can I ask you something?

145
00:12:11,791 --> 00:12:15,501
Depending on the reward I offer you,

146
00:12:15,581 --> 00:12:18,421
would you consider joining us in the war, Asser?

147
00:12:21,841 --> 00:12:23,761
Don't be silly!

148
00:12:23,841 --> 00:12:26,221
I know that a small country like us

149
00:12:26,301 --> 00:12:29,431
is no match for the king of Denmark!

150
00:12:32,431 --> 00:12:36,311
Pardon me. I was just checking.

151
00:12:38,191 --> 00:12:39,861
He's a weird guy.

152
00:12:40,941 --> 00:12:44,531
-Who?
-Askeladd, of course.

153
00:12:44,611 --> 00:12:47,661
I didn't know he could speak Welsh.

154
00:12:49,331 --> 00:12:52,751
I guess there's some things even you don't know.

155
00:12:52,831 --> 00:12:55,831
Idiot. There's lots I don't know.

156
00:12:55,921 --> 00:13:00,051
All I know is that he's from Denmark.

157
00:13:01,761 --> 00:13:03,631
He's such a pain.

158
00:13:03,721 --> 00:13:07,181
We've been together for decades,
but he never tells me anything.

159
00:13:08,551 --> 00:13:11,261
Are you sulking about it? I thought you're a grown man.

160
00:13:11,351 --> 00:13:13,931
Shut up! Are you trying to start a fight?

161
00:13:14,021 --> 00:13:16,481
Calm down.

162
00:13:16,561 --> 00:13:19,611
Who cares? He's a good leader.

163
00:13:19,691 --> 00:13:22,571
And luck is on his side.
As long as he lets me make money,

164
00:13:22,651 --> 00:13:25,491
I don't care where he's from. I'll follow him.

165
00:13:35,041 --> 00:13:38,331
That's hard to believe.

166
00:13:38,421 --> 00:13:40,791
But it's too unbelievable to be a lie.

167
00:13:40,881 --> 00:13:44,131
He's Lydia's child.

168
00:13:44,211 --> 00:13:50,181
Askeladd has inherited the blood of the great leaders
of the Roman Celts.

169
00:13:51,431 --> 00:13:54,931
Then you're not a Dane?

170
00:13:56,061 --> 00:13:58,601
I'm of mixed Danish and Welsh blood.

171
00:13:58,691 --> 00:14:02,611
My father Olaf conquered the coast of Wales
in the past

172
00:14:02,691 --> 00:14:06,531
and carried away Lydia as his concubine.

173
00:14:06,611 --> 00:14:08,951
I was their child.

174
00:14:10,321 --> 00:14:14,791
I had heard that the bloodline of the war gods
of Old Britannia had gone extinct.

175
00:14:16,581 --> 00:14:20,581
Are you saying you're a descendant of Artorius?

176
00:14:22,921 --> 00:14:25,011
Artorius

177
00:14:25,091 --> 00:14:28,341
was the commander of the Britannia army,

178
00:14:28,431 --> 00:14:31,471
a country of native Celtic inhabitants of England

179
00:14:31,551 --> 00:14:33,851
in the fifth and sixth centuries.

180
00:14:35,271 --> 00:14:36,981
In 516,

181
00:14:37,061 --> 00:14:39,391
he defeated the invading Saxons

182
00:14:39,481 --> 00:14:41,771
and the English

183
00:14:41,861 --> 00:14:44,651
at the Battle of Baden Hill

184
00:14:44,731 --> 00:14:47,321
and brought peace to Britannia.

185
00:14:48,781 --> 00:14:50,661
Legends about him were passed down.

186
00:14:50,741 --> 00:14:54,911
He was the person that the legend of King Arthur
was based on.

187
00:14:58,961 --> 00:15:00,961
Do you have any proof

188
00:15:01,041 --> 00:15:04,001
of your story?

189
00:15:04,091 --> 00:15:05,251
It's me.

190
00:15:05,341 --> 00:15:07,671
When he was 14,

191
00:15:07,761 --> 00:15:12,141
he visited Wales with Lydia and met me for the first time.

192
00:15:21,061 --> 00:15:23,311
She was an unlucky person.

193
00:15:23,401 --> 00:15:26,111
She was kept in my father's bedroom for a long time.

194
00:15:26,191 --> 00:15:29,281
After she became sick, she was kept in the stable.

195
00:15:30,281 --> 00:15:31,951
That's terrible.

196
00:15:33,571 --> 00:15:35,281
But who would ever imagine

197
00:15:35,371 --> 00:15:38,291
that the last remaining survivor of the great bloodline

198
00:15:38,371 --> 00:15:40,581
is mixed with Danish blood?

199
00:15:40,661 --> 00:15:42,831
That's exactly it, Asser.

200
00:15:42,921 --> 00:15:45,921
It's because I'm of mixed blood...

201
00:15:47,341 --> 00:15:49,711
I blend in with the Danes.

202
00:15:49,801 --> 00:15:53,181
In other words, Wales is going to have

203
00:15:53,261 --> 00:15:56,681
an insider in the army of Denmark, me.

204
00:15:56,761 --> 00:16:00,641
If I can make that rich boy into the next king of Denmark,

205
00:16:00,731 --> 00:16:02,981
then I can claim a high position too.

206
00:16:04,021 --> 00:16:06,521
If that happens,

207
00:16:06,611 --> 00:16:10,241
I can conclude a treaty of non-aggression with Wales.

208
00:16:14,491 --> 00:16:16,321
I won't let them...

209
00:16:16,411 --> 00:16:20,041
take even one step into this land.

210
00:16:28,091 --> 00:16:30,961
My goodness! That's so bold!

211
00:16:31,051 --> 00:16:33,341
You almost had me!

212
00:16:35,761 --> 00:16:40,181
-Asser is laughing.
-He's confident.

213
00:16:43,271 --> 00:16:45,311
I have one concern.

214
00:16:47,611 --> 00:16:50,901
You were raised among the Danes.

215
00:16:50,981 --> 00:16:55,701
Are you sure the blood of Artorius is stronger
than the blood of the Danes within you?

216
00:17:01,081 --> 00:17:03,791
You'll just have to believe me.

217
00:17:04,791 --> 00:17:07,331
Regardless of my blood...

218
00:17:10,591 --> 00:17:14,011
I hate the Danes.

219
00:17:17,841 --> 00:17:19,471
Put your swords here.

220
00:17:19,551 --> 00:17:21,721
You'll give them back, right?

221
00:17:22,771 --> 00:17:24,231
I refuse.

222
00:17:24,311 --> 00:17:26,691
I can't let go of my weapons!

223
00:17:29,401 --> 00:17:31,521
We're just pretending to be their prisoners

224
00:17:31,611 --> 00:17:34,401
while we pass through their country.

225
00:17:35,571 --> 00:17:39,371
They'll return our weapons when we get to the border.

226
00:17:39,451 --> 00:17:42,331
That way they won't lose face.

227
00:17:43,451 --> 00:17:49,331
That's strange.
Why do your men follow your orders?

228
00:17:50,791 --> 00:17:52,501
It's amazing, right?

229
00:17:52,591 --> 00:17:56,131
That's because my men trust me.

230
00:17:56,221 --> 00:17:58,181
Hey, Thorfinn. You too.

231
00:17:58,261 --> 00:17:59,591
Don't mess with me, baldy.

232
00:18:00,641 --> 00:18:01,851
You said trust?

233
00:18:01,931 --> 00:18:05,181
He's different. You damn kid.

234
00:18:05,271 --> 00:18:07,941
Don't get the wrong idea, conehead.

235
00:18:08,021 --> 00:18:11,821
I'm not one of his men. I'm an enemy.

236
00:18:11,901 --> 00:18:12,821
An enemy?

237
00:18:12,901 --> 00:18:16,611
Fine. So be it. You two don't have to obey.

238
00:18:16,691 --> 00:18:19,741
It's only the two of you. I'm sure they'll allow it.

239
00:18:21,201 --> 00:18:24,411
All right! Let's start marching again!

240
00:18:31,081 --> 00:18:32,381
Danes?

241
00:18:32,461 --> 00:18:35,511
I heard Asser captured them!

242
00:18:42,971 --> 00:18:46,771
-I heard they eat humans!
-They look evil!

243
00:18:50,231 --> 00:18:52,231
Asser is amazing.

244
00:18:52,311 --> 00:18:53,611
Let me try throwing a stone.

245
00:18:53,691 --> 00:18:54,771
Stop, you idiot!

246
00:18:54,861 --> 00:18:57,361
They piss me off.

247
00:18:57,441 --> 00:18:59,281
They're treating us like animals.

248
00:18:59,361 --> 00:19:01,991
You better not look at us!

249
00:19:02,071 --> 00:19:03,701
We're going to eat you!

250
00:19:14,131 --> 00:19:16,461
Don't look, princess.

251
00:19:17,631 --> 00:19:21,011
If there's something you want to say,
say it yourself.

252
00:19:21,091 --> 00:19:23,301
Don't you have a tongue?

253
00:19:30,181 --> 00:19:31,351
I...

254
00:19:31,441 --> 00:19:32,441
I...

255
00:19:33,941 --> 00:19:37,231
The reason I don't talk isn't because
I'm a coward.

256
00:19:38,901 --> 00:19:41,861
I-- I'm cautious.

257
00:19:41,951 --> 00:19:44,121
I have to be cautious.

258
00:19:45,201 --> 00:19:46,871
I'm the prince.

259
00:19:46,951 --> 00:19:49,871
I'm different from you.

260
00:19:52,041 --> 00:19:56,751
My words have political repercussions.

261
00:19:57,881 --> 00:20:00,051
Especially in a foreign country.

262
00:20:00,131 --> 00:20:02,931
Without permission from my father, the king,

263
00:20:03,011 --> 00:20:07,391
I can't enter a treaty of non-aggression,

264
00:20:07,471 --> 00:20:09,181
or say words to make them uncomfortable.

265
00:20:16,731 --> 00:20:19,571
I'm cautious.

266
00:20:21,651 --> 00:20:24,361
Not bad for an excuse.

267
00:20:24,451 --> 00:20:27,331
It looks like you're smart enough, princess.

268
00:20:28,621 --> 00:20:31,451
How dare you!

269
00:20:31,541 --> 00:20:33,501
This is the first time!

270
00:20:33,581 --> 00:20:36,381
No one's treated me like this before!

271
00:20:36,461 --> 00:20:38,801
Then it was a good experience, right?

272
00:20:38,881 --> 00:20:42,221
Take it back! It's not an excuse.

273
00:20:42,301 --> 00:20:44,971
You don't understand how hard it is to be
from the royal family!

274
00:20:45,051 --> 00:20:47,221
Stop barking. Sit down.

275
00:20:47,301 --> 00:20:49,261
I'm not barking!

276
00:20:49,351 --> 00:20:52,561
I'm just declaring that I'm right!

277
00:20:52,641 --> 00:20:54,231
Okay, I understand.

278
00:20:54,311 --> 00:20:57,481
What's wrong with your attitude?

279
00:20:57,561 --> 00:21:01,611
I can't believe His Highness is talking to someone
other than me!

280
00:21:01,691 --> 00:21:02,941
I'm not your servant.

281
00:21:45,031 --> 00:21:45,861
Snow.

282
00:21:53,701 --> 00:21:57,621
Askeladd! Can't you go any faster?

283
00:21:57,711 --> 00:22:00,541
We're going to be trapped in the snow!

284
00:22:00,631 --> 00:22:04,131
Enough already. He's such a pain.

285
00:22:06,011 --> 00:22:08,891
We're going to cross the northern edge of Mercia

286
00:22:08,971 --> 00:22:11,801
and head for the main army in Gainsborough.

287
00:22:11,891 --> 00:22:14,061
We'll arrive in one week.

288
00:22:14,141 --> 00:22:16,891
You mean we'll go through enemy territory?

289
00:22:16,981 --> 00:22:21,481
If we get trapped in the mountains of Wales in the snow,
we'll be stuck.

290
00:22:22,651 --> 00:22:25,441
What's wrong? Don't worry.

291
00:22:25,531 --> 00:22:27,701
We're far away from the enemy's main camp.

292
00:22:27,781 --> 00:22:30,411
But how are you going to get supplies?

293
00:22:30,491 --> 00:22:34,331
The lords in the countryside aren't going to
help out a Dane!

294
00:22:34,411 --> 00:22:36,201
It'll be fine.

295
00:22:36,291 --> 00:22:38,661
I've thought about that already.

296
00:22:42,421 --> 00:22:46,211
Men! We're changing directions! With haste!

297
00:22:46,301 --> 00:22:48,971
-What?
-Really?

298
00:22:49,051 --> 00:22:51,221
We're changing routes all of a sudden?

299
00:22:51,301 --> 00:22:55,811
Don't worry! Askeladd's decision isn't wrong!
